WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.503
Teacher midterm practice for C plus plus, question 14.

2
00:00:05.123 --> 00:00:08.103
This code is meant to print the minimum of A equals three

3
00:00:08.103 --> 00:00:11.710
point five, two, eight point one, four point four.

4
00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:13.540
What does it actually print?

5
00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.764
m starts at zero. Then each element is compared with m.

6
00:00:19.385 --> 00:00:24.883
Three point five less than zero? No. Two? No. Eight point one? No. Four point four?

7
00:00:24.883 --> 00:00:25.299
No.

8
00:00:25.919 --> 00:00:30.621
m never changes, so it prints zero: a value that is not even in the array.

9
00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.517
A running minimum must start from a real element.

10
00:00:35.517 --> 00:00:39.756
Start with m equal to A of zero, then loop from i equals one.

11
00:00:39.756 --> 00:00:42.700
The same rule applies to a running maximum.

12
00:00:44.301 --> 00:00:45.408
So it prints zero.

13
00:00:46.228 --> 00:00:49.203
Initialising a minimum or maximum to zero.

14
00:00:49.203 --> 00:00:53.089
It fails whenever all the values are on one side of zero.

15
00:00:53.809 --> 00:00:57.698
Exam tip: when a min or max question gives all positive, or

16
00:00:57.698 --> 00:01:03.328
all negative values, check the starting value first. That is usually the trick.
